WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.040
Follow the same payment
test through six handoffs.

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:06.480
The approved requirement says
a retry with the same key

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:09.840
returns the original transfer
and creates no second posting.

00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:13.440
The reviewed test matrix
carries that intent forward.

00:00:14.187 --> 00:00:16.240
AI drafts a candidate test.

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:20.880
A response-status check alone is too weak,
so the reviewer requires the original

00:00:20.924 --> 00:00:25.280
transfer identifier, one journal,
and independently agreed balances.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:28.800
That reviewed test becomes
the change we can inspect.

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:32.720
Now challenge the test
with a deliberately faulty build.

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:36.880
Our illustrative run finds two
journals where one was expected.

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:40.320
The test fails, and the original
failure evidence is retained.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:44.880
AI links the failed assertion
to the journal and trace evidence,

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:47.600
and proposes
a duplicate-posting diagnosis.

00:00:47.813 --> 00:00:51.520
An engineer confirms the cause
and reviews the proposed fix.

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:54.400
The pipeline performs a fresh retest.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:58.880
The corrected build must pass, while
the deliberately faulty variant must still

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:04.400
fail. Changing the test simply to accept
two debits would invalidate the result.

00:01:04.985 --> 00:01:10.000
Finally, the release pack links the
requirement, reviewed test, failed run,

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:14.560
fix and retest. The release owner checks
remaining coverage and real-provider

00:01:14.607 --> 00:01:19.440
integration evidence. A passing
retest is an input to that decision,

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:24.080
not automatic approval. All identifiers
and results here are authored examples.
